          'Ten years after the fall of Troy, every other surviving Greek captain has reached home and Odysseus alone is still missing. On Ithaca his absence has become an occupation: a crowd of young noblemen camps in his hall, slaughters his herds, courts his wife Penelope, and waits for her to admit that she is a widow. The household is being eaten alive, and the poem opens on that slow domestic emergency rather than on the sea.\n\nHomer gives the first four books not to Odysseus but to his son. Telemachus was an infant when his father sailed, and he has grown up powerless in a house full of louder men. Athena, disguised as a family friend, goads him into calling an assembly and then into sailing out to learn what he can. At Pylos he questions old Nestor, and at Sparta he sits with Menelaus and Helen, who tell him at last that his father is alive and held on a distant island.\n\nThe detour is not a delay but a foundation. By the time Odysseus finally appears, the reader knows exactly what is at stake on Ithaca and has watched a boy begin turning into a man worth coming home to. It also establishes the epic’s deepest measure of worth, which is not conquest but the ability to hold a household and a name together.',
