        body:
          'The moral spine of the epic is "xenia," the sacred obligation between host and guest that the gods themselves enforce. Almost every episode tests it. The Phaeacians feed, clothe, and convey a naked stranger home without asking anything in return, and they are honored for it; Polyphemus eats his guests and is blinded; the suitors devour their absent host’s property for years and are killed to the last man.\n\nAgainst that standard the poem sorts everyone in Ithaca into the loyal and the false. The swineherd Eumaeus shares his food with a beggar he has no reason to help. The nurse Eurycleia recognizes the scar and holds her tongue. The old dog Argos, lying neglected on a dung heap, lifts his head at his master’s voice and dies. Beside them stand the goatherd Melanthius and the maids who took the suitors as lovers, and the poem gives them no mercy at all.\n\nWhat looks like a revenge plot is therefore also a restoration of order. The slaughter in the hall is violent enough to unsettle modern readers, but Homer frames it as the correction of a world turned upside down, in which guests behaved like thieves and a household ran without its head. The peace Athena imposes at the very end confirms that the point was never blood. It was the return of a house to its proper shape.',
