    aboutAuthor:
      'Homer is the name antiquity attached to the two great Greek epics, *The Iliad* and *The Odyssey*, which most scholars place in roughly the eighth century BCE. Almost nothing about him can be established. Ancient tradition made him a blind singer from somewhere in the Greek-speaking eastern Aegean, but that portrait is legend rather than record, and the poems themselves say nothing at all about their author.\n\nModern scholarship has largely reframed the question. Study of living oral traditions showed that singers can compose enormous narrative poems in performance using inherited formulas, and the repeated epithets and set phrases of the Homeric epics look exactly like the tools such a singer would use. Many scholars therefore see the poems as the culmination of a long collective tradition, shaped by generations of performers before being fixed in writing, while others still argue for a single organizing intelligence behind the design. The debate has its own name, the "Homeric Question," and it remains unsettled.\n\nWhat is not in doubt is the influence. The epics were the backbone of ancient Greek education and were revered in Rome as well, where Virgil wrote the *Aeneid* in conscious response to them. Their reach continued through Dante, through centuries of English translation, and into the modern novel, most famously in James Joyce’s *Ulysses*, which maps the wanderings of Odysseus onto a single day in Dublin. Few authors, real or composite, have cast a longer shadow.',
