        body:
          'Okonkwo is a self-made man in the nine villages of Umuofia, famous since the age of eighteen for throwing the wrestler Amalinze the Cat, and by middle age he holds two titles, three wives, and full barns. All of it has been built against a memory. His father Unoka was a gentle, flute-playing debtor who died without a title, and Okonkwo has organized his entire personality around not being him.\n\nAchebe spends the first third of the novel simply showing how this world works, and the patience of it is the point. The kola nut is broken before serious conversation begins, disputes go before the masked egwugwu, and the Week of Peace suspends violence and fines anyone who breaks it, as Okonkwo is fined when he beats a wife during it. Proverbs carry arguments, festivals mark the year, and the Oracle of the Hills and the Caves speaks with an authority no man questions.\n\nInto this household comes Ikemefuna, a boy handed over by a neighboring village as compensation for a murder. He lives with Okonkwo for three years, calls him father, and becomes the older brother that Okonkwo’s son Nwoye badly needs. The reader is allowed to grow fond of him, which is exactly the trap the novel is setting.',
