    aboutAuthor:
      'Emily Brontë (1818–1848) spent almost her entire life in the parsonage at Haworth, a village on the edge of the West Yorkshire moors, where her father was the perpetual curate. Her mother died when she was three, and she grew up with her sisters Charlotte and Anne and her brother Branwell in a house full of books, isolation, and invented worlds. With Anne she developed an elaborate imaginary kingdom called Gondal and wrote poems set in it for years. The moorland that begins at that door is the landscape of the novel.\n\nShe was intensely private, unhappy away from home, and by every account the least sociable member of a shy family. In 1846 the three sisters published a joint volume of poems under the deliberately ambiguous names Currer, Ellis, and Acton Bell, chosen so that their work would not be judged as women’s writing, and it sold almost no copies. *Wuthering Heights* appeared the following year under the name Ellis Bell, and reviewers were bewildered by it, calling it powerful, coarse, and disagreeable in roughly equal measure.\n\nEmily caught cold at her brother’s funeral in the autumn of 1848 and died of tuberculosis that December at the age of thirty, refusing medical help almost to the end. She never saw her novel treated as anything more than a curiosity, and Charlotte felt obliged to write an apologetic preface for a later edition, explaining her sister to a public that had recoiled. The reputation that arrived afterward has been extraordinary. The single book Emily left is now read as one of the most original novels in the language, valued for precisely the wildness that appalled the people who reviewed it first.',
