        meaning:
          'Sethe cannot see her own back and has no feeling left in it, so the scars exist for her only in other people’s descriptions: a chokecherry tree to Amy Denver, a piece of wrought sculpture to Paul D, a revolting clump of scars to whoever is less kind. That gap between the mark and the woman carrying it is the point. Slavery wrote on her body in a script she is unable to read, so she has to receive her own history from whoever happens to be looking. When Paul D kisses the scars and she feels nothing at all, Morrison shows exactly how far the numbness goes.',
