    aboutAuthor:
      'Toni Morrison (1931–2019) was born Chloe Wofford in Lorain, Ohio, a steel town where her father worked as a welder and her family told stories, sang, and carried with them the folklore of the South they had left. She studied at Howard University and at Cornell, taught for several years, and then moved into publishing, where as an editor at Random House she spent nearly two decades bringing Black writers and Black histories into print. She began writing fiction in the margins of that working life, publishing *The Bluest Eye* in 1970 while raising two sons on her own.\n\nHer subject from the beginning was the interior life of Black Americans, written without an explanatory glance over the shoulder at a white reader. *Song of Solomon* brought her national recognition, and *Beloved*, published in 1987, made her a central figure in American letters. She had come across the case of Margaret Garner, an enslaved woman who killed her own daughter rather than see her taken back into slavery, while working on a documentary anthology years earlier, and the story stayed with her until she found a form equal to it.\n\n*Beloved* won the Pulitzer Prize and contributed substantially to the Nobel Prize in Literature she received in 1993, the first ever awarded to a Black woman. She went on writing novels and essays for another quarter century, taught for many years at Princeton, and remained a formidable public voice on race, language, and the responsibilities of literature. Her books are among the most widely taught in American schools and among the most frequently challenged.',
