        motivation:
          'Nanny is trying to secure the only thing she believes a Black woman in her world can hope for, which is protection: a roof, a legal husband, sixty acres, and no man able to use her granddaughter as she and her own daughter were used. Everything she does comes out of having been enslaved and having watched her daughter destroyed. What she wants for Janie is not happiness, which she regards as a luxury she has never seen, but safety.',
        arc:
          'She raises Janie with fierce love, marries her off at sixteen, and dies not long afterward, convinced that she has done her duty. Janie spends decades working out that her grandmother mistook a high seat for a life, and comes to feel that Nanny took the horizon, the biggest thing God ever made, and pinched it small enough to tie around her granddaughter’s neck. The novel treats her with real sympathy even as it rejects her advice, because her fear was earned. She stands for a generation whose survival strategy became the next generation’s cage.',
